<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Babenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">976–<a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Babenberg dynasty, Babenbergs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Babenberg ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 976, first as margraves and from 1156 as dukes, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-minus">Privilegium Minus</a> raised the march to a duchy. Leopold I was the first margrave, and his descendants moved their seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in 1192. The line ended when Frederick II the Quarrelsome was killed in battle in 1246, opening the way for Ottokar II and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg.jpg" alt="House of Babenberg" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Archduchy_of_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
